# The user guide's pictures, and how take-pictures.sh makes each one.
#
# `session <sample>` starts the editor afresh, in a new home folder, on a
# new copy of a sample (game, ui, playground or benchmark; `none` for no
# project). `picture <file>` starts a picture; the indented lines under it
# say which page shows it, what it shows, and the steps that make it, in
# order. A picture carries on from the one above it in its session.
#
# Steps (places are pixels on the 1440 x 900 screen, standard layout):
#   key <keys>            press keys (xdotool's names: Escape, Tab, space, b)
#   type <words>          type words
#   move X Y              move the pointer there
#   click X Y             click there; `double X Y` double-clicks, `right X Y` right-clicks
#   drag X Y TO_X TO_Y    drag with the left button
#   wait <seconds>        wait (the shot waits for the screen to hold still anyway)
#   write <file> <text>   write a file into the project (\n for a new line)
#   prepend <file> <text> put text at the start of a file of the project
#   restart [sample]      start the editor again in the same home folder,
#                         after quitting it with File > Exit (on a new
#                         copy of a sample, when one is named)
#   label X Y <n>         a numbered mark on the picture at X Y
#   shot X Y W H [width]  take the picture: the screen from X Y, W by H,
#                         scaled to `width` pixels across when given
#
# Prefer the search (key space, type, key Return) to clicks: it finds an
# action wherever the layout puts its button.

session playground

